 Javascript is synchronus and single threaded

other multithreaded languages are Java, C#, Python, etc

 Asynchronous programming allows us to perform long-running tasks without blocking the main thread of execution.
 This is particularly important in JavaScript, which is single-threaded, meaning it can only execute one task at a time.


 **** Blocking code and Non-blocking code ****

 Blocking code is code that prevents the execution of other code until it has completed.

 Non-blocking code, on the other hand, allows other code to execute while it is still running. 

This is achieved through the use of callbacks, promises, and async/await syntax in JavaScript.


Task Queue:- 
          The task queue is a data structure that holds tasks that are waiting to be executed. 
          When the main thread is idle, it will check the task queue for any pending tasks and execute them in order.    
          When an asynchronous operation is initiated, such as a network request or a timer, it is added to the task queue. Once the main thread has completed its current task, it will check the task queue and 
          execute any pending tasks.


Promises:- 
          A promise is an object that represents the eventual completion (or failure) of an asynchronous operation and its resulting value. 
          Promises provide a cleaner and more readable way to handle asynchronous code compared to callbacks. 
          They allow you to chain multiple asynchronous operations together and handle errors more effectively.

Q:- Why promises are called high priority tasks/queue?
A:- Promises are often considered high priority tasks because they are designed to handle asynchronous operations that may take some time to complete, such as network requests or file I/O. 
When a promise is created, it is added to the microtask queue, which has a higher priority than the regular task queue.

How to make javscript code asynchronous?
There are several ways to make JavaScript code asynchronous:
1. Callbacks: You can use callbacks to execute a function after an asynchronous operation has completed. 
   For example, you can use the setTimeout function to execute a callback after a specified delay.
2. Promises: You can use promises to handle asynchronous operations in a more structured way. 
   Promises allow you to chain multiple asynchronous operations together and handle errors more effectively.
3. Async/Await: You can use async/await syntax to write asynchronous code that looks and behaves like synchronous code.
    The async keyword is used to declare a function as asynchronous, and the await keyword is used to wait for a promise to resolve before continuing with the execution of the code. 
    This makes it easier to read and understand asynchronous code, as it eliminates the need for nested callbacks or complex promise chains.

set timeout example using callbacks
basically setTimeout is a built-in JavaScript function that allows you to execute a function after a specified delay.


******** fetch api ********
 fetch() api is latest in JavaScript for making network requests. 
 It returns a promise that resolves to the response of the request.

 it works on the task queue and is non-blocking, 
 basicall it allows you to create a high priority task that will be executed as soon as the current task is completed,
 meaning it allows other code to execute while waiting for the response.



